  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $\u27e8x,-2\u27e9$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ 2x+y=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-6$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-12$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=3$",
  "worked_solution": "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|}{3} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
